WEBVTT

00:03.920 --> 00:04.280
All right.

00:04.320 --> 00:06.160
So now what is a nested CTE?

00:06.320 --> 00:09.000
It is a CTE inside another city.

00:09.200 --> 00:10.840
So it's kind of like subqueries.

00:10.880 --> 00:12.880
A query inside another query.

00:12.880 --> 00:16.120
So not only a main query can use the result of CTE.

00:16.640 --> 00:19.080
Another CTE can use the results from a CTE.

00:19.280 --> 00:21.920
And of course the nested city is like a mini query.

00:21.920 --> 00:24.040
It's depend on other queries.

00:24.040 --> 00:28.880
That means you cannot go and select it and run it independently from the query.

00:28.880 --> 00:34.720
So always you have to run the CTE inside it first before seeing the result of that nested CTE.

00:34.760 --> 00:35.000
Okay.

00:35.040 --> 00:36.720
So now let's understand what this means.

00:36.760 --> 00:38.160
Again we have our database.

00:38.160 --> 00:43.960
And we have a city query that goes directly to the database and queries the data from there.

00:44.000 --> 00:47.160
And in the output we will get the intermediate results.

00:47.200 --> 00:52.640
And now in this scenario this time we will not have only one intermediate results because we have many

00:52.640 --> 00:53.680
different steps.

00:53.680 --> 00:58.960
We need another intermediate results before everything is prepared for the main query.

00:58.960 --> 01:05.220
So that means we have another step that's going to be built up on top of the first intermediate results.

01:05.220 --> 01:11.500
So that means we can have another CTE that's going to be querying the results from the first CTE and

01:11.500 --> 01:14.620
built on top of it another intermediate result.

01:14.620 --> 01:17.980
So as you can see here we have CTE one and CTE two.

01:18.100 --> 01:21.340
And that means we now we have like two intermediate results.

01:21.340 --> 01:25.100
And now of course we can go and add CTE three four and so on.

01:25.100 --> 01:31.300
But now let's say that the CTE two going to prepare the final intermediate result for the main query.

01:31.300 --> 01:35.460
So now the main query is going to go and query the second intermediate results.

01:35.460 --> 01:40.100
And it's going to do the final step where the final result can be presented for the user.

01:40.100 --> 01:46.100
And of course if it is needed, the main query can access not only the second intermediate results from

01:46.100 --> 01:50.820
the second CTE, but also the first intermediate result from the CTE one.

01:50.860 --> 01:57.730
Now we call the first CTE a standalone CTE because it doesn't depend on any intermediate results It

01:57.730 --> 02:00.410
goes directly to the database and gets the data.

02:00.410 --> 02:05.690
But now since the second CTE is completely depending on the CTE one.

02:05.730 --> 02:11.130
So this time we're going to call this CTE a nested CTE because we cannot go and execute it on its own.

02:11.130 --> 02:13.250
It always depends on the city one.

02:13.290 --> 02:15.970
And of course the main city is depending on everything.

02:15.970 --> 02:17.850
So as you can see we're using the cities.

02:17.890 --> 02:19.810
We're going to go and build like a chain.

02:19.810 --> 02:24.290
So this is what we mean with that standalone CTE and nested CTE okay.

02:24.330 --> 02:26.970
So now let's understand the syntax of the nested CTE.

02:27.010 --> 02:32.730
So we start as usual with the definition of the first city using the with clause and then the name of

02:32.730 --> 02:35.090
the city and the definition of the city.

02:35.130 --> 02:36.570
So here it's nothing new.

02:36.810 --> 02:43.010
Now we go and define the second CTE as we learned using the comma, then the name of the CTE and the

02:43.010 --> 02:43.770
definition.

02:43.770 --> 02:46.010
So this is our CTE number two.

02:46.050 --> 02:50.650
So now the second CTE is depending on the results of the first CTE.

02:50.810 --> 02:51.770
So how are we going to do it.

02:51.770 --> 02:52.730
It's very simple.

02:52.970 --> 02:58.270
Now for the CTE number two we're going to select the data from the city number one.

02:58.270 --> 03:02.750
And with that we are making the second city depending on the first one.

03:02.750 --> 03:08.030
So this means the second city is getting the data from the first one, and it's querying the data in

03:08.030 --> 03:09.670
order to do the second step.

03:09.710 --> 03:16.230
And with that, we are nesting one city in another, and the city two is completely depending on the

03:16.230 --> 03:16.950
first one.

03:16.950 --> 03:23.070
So again, we call the first CTE as standalone CTE because it doesn't depend on anything.

03:23.110 --> 03:27.670
We can execute it on its own and it just needs the data directly from the database.

03:27.670 --> 03:34.070
But the second CTE, since it's completely dependent on the city number one, we call it a nested CTE.

03:34.270 --> 03:35.310
So they are very similar.

03:35.310 --> 03:38.590
We are just selecting the data from the city number one.

03:38.630 --> 03:40.350
And now comes our main query.

03:40.350 --> 03:44.710
And of course it's going to go and use the data from the second step.

03:44.710 --> 03:47.830
So it's going to go and select the data from the city number two.

03:47.870 --> 03:49.990
But it's still of course it's not a rule.

03:49.990 --> 03:54.030
It can go and access the data and select the data from the city number one.

03:54.050 --> 03:57.610
So this is how we can create a nested CTE in SQL.

03:57.730 --> 04:02.090
All right guys back to our project where we are creating a report about the customers.

04:02.090 --> 04:04.090
And we would like to add one more step.

04:04.090 --> 04:09.610
So the task is rank the customers based on total sales per customer.

04:09.610 --> 04:12.290
So this is one more step inside our projects.

04:12.290 --> 04:17.490
And we would like to go and use as well the CTE in order to implement this step.

04:17.490 --> 04:18.370
So now what do we need.

04:18.410 --> 04:22.450
We need to rank the customers based on total sales for each customer.

04:22.450 --> 04:24.330
So here like we have two steps.

04:24.370 --> 04:27.050
First we have to calculate the total sales per customer.

04:27.050 --> 04:30.890
And then we have to go and rank it based on this information.

04:30.890 --> 04:34.010
And of course the sales are stored inside the orders.

04:34.010 --> 04:36.330
So now let's go and start implementing the CTE.

04:36.690 --> 04:44.050
So we're going to have a comma and we're going to call it CTE customer rank as.

04:44.090 --> 04:46.610
And then we're going to go have the parentheses.

04:46.610 --> 04:49.210
And inside it we're going to develop now the logic.

04:49.210 --> 04:53.080
So first we have to go and aggregate the data by the total sales.

04:53.080 --> 05:03.280
So select customer ID and then sum the sales from the table sales orders.

05:03.280 --> 05:07.480
And then of course group by the customer ID.

05:07.720 --> 05:11.360
And now I can hear you even telling me we have already done this.

05:11.360 --> 05:12.560
We have already this logic.

05:12.560 --> 05:18.200
So why we are repeating if we go to the first CTE, you can see we have already done that.

05:18.200 --> 05:19.520
And you are totally right.

05:19.520 --> 05:23.400
We have already the logic, so it makes no sense to repeat it again.

05:23.440 --> 05:27.600
And if we do this, then we didn't understood the power of the CTE.

05:27.760 --> 05:34.120
So we don't have to repeat the same logic and we can reuse the CTE inside another CTE.

05:34.280 --> 05:36.120
So now we don't need all those stuff.

05:36.120 --> 05:39.960
We can go and focus immediately with ranking the customers.

05:39.960 --> 05:43.160
So first let me just select the data from the first CTE.

05:43.520 --> 05:45.120
So I'm going to go and select.

05:45.120 --> 05:45.880
So what do we have.

05:45.880 --> 05:55.300
We have customer ID and we have total sales and we're going to select it this time, not from any physical

05:55.340 --> 05:55.660
table.

05:55.660 --> 05:57.740
We're going to select our CTE.

05:57.940 --> 05:59.060
So like this.

05:59.100 --> 06:03.220
And now what we're going to do we're going to go and select the whole thing and execute it.

06:03.260 --> 06:06.740
Well this is the issue of nesting cities.

06:06.980 --> 06:11.300
Sadly this city is completely depending on the fair city.

06:11.300 --> 06:13.820
So we cannot go and execute it on its own.

06:13.820 --> 06:19.940
And this is of course very annoying because each time I execute the query by the end of the query,

06:19.940 --> 06:23.700
it's going to go and destroy all the ctes.

06:23.740 --> 06:26.740
So in the memory we will not find the CTE.

06:26.900 --> 06:32.220
And that's why once I executed it, SQL don't know anything about this city.

06:32.220 --> 06:39.260
And in order now to see the result of this, we have always to execute as well with it the CTE that

06:39.260 --> 06:40.020
I'm using.

06:40.020 --> 06:47.260
So what I usually do, I go over here and make everything in comment in the main query, and now I can

06:47.260 --> 06:49.050
go and execute the whole thing.

06:49.090 --> 06:53.090
And now I will see in the output the outcome of this necessity.

06:53.130 --> 06:58.330
So this is the big difference between the standalone CDs like here and the nested.

06:58.370 --> 06:59.730
So now let's go back to our task.

06:59.730 --> 07:03.370
We have to rank those sales based on the total sales.

07:03.370 --> 07:08.410
So we can go and use the rank function from the window function.

07:08.410 --> 07:10.170
So rank over.

07:10.930 --> 07:12.890
And now we don't have to partition the data.

07:12.890 --> 07:18.370
We just want to sort the data by the total sales descending.

07:19.610 --> 07:23.130
So like this the highest sales can I get the rank number one.

07:23.130 --> 07:27.210
So let's go and give it the name as customer rank.

07:27.490 --> 07:31.050
Now as you can see we have a really nice rank beside those informations.

07:31.050 --> 07:35.810
Customer three has the highest sales and customer two has the lowest total sales.

07:35.810 --> 07:38.770
So that as you can see we didn't repeat ourselves.

07:38.770 --> 07:42.970
We just reused another city in our current city.

07:43.010 --> 07:49.110
And this is exactly why this technique is very amazing in order to reduce redundancies and to reduce

07:49.110 --> 07:51.110
the complexity of the whole query.

07:51.110 --> 07:56.790
So nested are annoying to execute, but they reduce the redundancies of our code.

07:56.830 --> 07:58.350
Now we are done with our logic.

07:58.350 --> 07:59.510
We tested everything.

07:59.510 --> 08:00.390
So what are we going to do?

08:00.390 --> 08:03.710
We're going to go and integrate it in our main query.

08:03.710 --> 08:08.750
So let me just remove the comments from here and let's go and add it in the main query.

08:08.750 --> 08:10.550
So we will do the same thing.

08:10.550 --> 08:15.190
We're going to go and do a left join with the last city that we just created.

08:15.190 --> 08:19.550
So let me just call it CCR and the same conditions.

08:19.550 --> 08:25.350
We are always joining on the customer ID, but don't forget to rename the alias.

08:25.510 --> 08:31.030
So it is CCR customer ID equal to the customer ID from the first table.

08:31.030 --> 08:34.670
And of course we have to go and select the new information.

08:34.670 --> 08:37.910
So CCR dot customer rank.

08:38.790 --> 08:41.270
And now let's go and execute the whole thing.

08:41.310 --> 08:45.850
Now as you can see in the results those three columns comes from the customers table.

08:45.890 --> 08:53.330
The total sales comes from the first CTE, the last order from the second CTE, and the customer rank

08:53.370 --> 08:56.850
comes from our nested CTE that we just created.

08:56.850 --> 09:03.570
So guys, it is not a simple task creating such a report because it involves different aggregations

09:03.570 --> 09:04.930
and different functions.

09:04.930 --> 09:06.610
But our work is organized.

09:06.610 --> 09:08.250
As you can see it's very simple.

09:08.250 --> 09:11.450
We have step one, step two, step three and the main query.

09:11.450 --> 09:15.530
And it's really easy to add more components to our query.

09:15.570 --> 09:19.170
Now I would like really to keep practicing using those nested queries.

09:19.170 --> 09:20.450
So we have the following task.

09:20.450 --> 09:26.970
We would like to add one more step in our report segment the customers based on their total sales.

09:26.970 --> 09:30.130
So I would like to implement this as well using CTE.

09:30.490 --> 09:31.930
So let's go and solve it.

09:31.970 --> 09:33.970
We want to go and add a new CTE.

09:34.010 --> 09:39.690
It's going to be CTE customer segments as.

09:39.730 --> 09:42.850
And then we have to go and define our logic.

09:42.850 --> 09:45.640
Now if you check our task, it has two parts.

09:45.640 --> 09:47.320
We have to find the total sales.

09:47.520 --> 09:51.360
And then we have to segment the customers based on this information.

09:51.360 --> 09:55.720
So it is something very similar to what we have done in the step three.

09:55.720 --> 10:00.720
So that means we don't have to go and calculate again the total sales we have to go and use as well

10:00.760 --> 10:02.760
our amazing first CTE.

10:02.800 --> 10:04.320
So let's go and do it.

10:05.240 --> 10:05.800
What do we need.

10:05.800 --> 10:09.040
We need the customer ID like this.

10:09.840 --> 10:13.000
And let's do basic segmentations using the casewhen.

10:13.240 --> 10:17.360
So let's say casewhen the total sales.

10:17.360 --> 10:23.400
If it's higher than 100, then let's say the customer is going to belong to the group high.

10:23.800 --> 10:26.600
And let's go and add another category.

10:27.080 --> 10:36.600
If it's not higher than 100, if it is higher than 50, then the customer is going to belong to medium.

10:37.240 --> 10:40.720
And if the total sales is less or equal to 50.

10:41.040 --> 10:42.300
So what's going to happen?

10:42.300 --> 10:46.620
We're going to say else the customer belongs to the low category.

10:47.060 --> 10:47.940
So that's it.

10:47.980 --> 10:52.100
We're going to have an end and let's call it customer segments.

10:52.500 --> 10:53.220
All right.

10:53.220 --> 10:56.060
But of course we have to go and select it from a table.

10:56.060 --> 10:58.100
And it's going to be our CTE.

10:58.140 --> 11:00.220
So total sales.

11:00.780 --> 11:02.940
And let's put it in our new CD.

11:03.060 --> 11:07.100
And I would like to test it before like putting it inside our main query.

11:07.180 --> 11:13.900
That's why I will put everything in comments in my main query since it is a nested CTE sadly.

11:13.900 --> 11:19.660
And we will just go and select our new nested CTE like we have done before.

11:19.780 --> 11:21.780
So let's go and execute it.

11:21.820 --> 11:27.900
Now as you can see in the output, we have two customers with the category high and two customers with

11:27.900 --> 11:28.660
the medium.

11:28.660 --> 11:32.660
But in order to make sure that everything is working perfectly, I would like to go and add the total

11:32.660 --> 11:34.820
sales just to see the numbers.

11:34.820 --> 11:36.140
So let's go and execute it.

11:36.180 --> 11:38.140
Well, you can see everything is correct.

11:38.140 --> 11:44.960
So those customers having higher than 100 in the total sales, and those two having higher than 50.

11:45.000 --> 11:46.680
